  all.

  src/
    model/task.js            Task shape + validation. Pure, no deps.
    store/storage.js         localStorage read/write, schemaVersion, migration hook.
    store/taskStore.js       In-memory state + mutations; emits change events.
    notifications/
      schedule.js            PURE core: (tasks, settings, now) -> due reminder events
      engine.js              Timer loop + fired-state bookkeeping; calls schedule.js
      delivery/index.js      Fan-out to channels behind one deliver(event) interface
      delivery/inPage.js     Toast + notification list
      delivery/osNotify.js   Notification API wrapper, permission, capability detection
    ui/taskForm.js  ui/taskList.js  ui/settings.js
    main.js                  Wiring only

  The boundaries that matter:

  - [1m[38;5;153mschedule.js[39m knows nothing about time passing.[0m It's handed a [38;5;153mnow[39m and returns which reminders are due. No timers, no
    storage, no DOM. Every interesting behavior — lead time, suppression, already-fired — is testable by calling one
    function with different numbers.
  - [1m[38;5;153mengine.js[39m is the only thing that owns a timer[0m and the only thing that writes fired-state.
  - [1m[38;5;153mdelivery/*[39m share one interface[0m, [38;5;153mdeliver(event)[39m. [38;5;153mosNotify[39m failing (no permission, unsupported browser) is invisible
    to the engine — it's a channel that declines. That's what keeps the OS layer a genuine enhancement rather than a
    second code path.
  - [1mThe UI never talks to the notification engine.[0m It talks to [38;5;153mtaskStore[39m; the engine observes the store.

  One deliberate choice worth flagging: [1ma single recurring tick (~15s) that recomputes from the clock, not one [0m
  [1m[38;5;153msetTimeout[39m per reminder.[0m Per-task timers drift, die on laptop sleep, and get throttled in background tabs — you'd wake
  from sleep and silently get nothing. Recomputing against [38;5;153mDate.now()[39m is self-healing: whatever was missed is simply
  found on the next tick. It also recomputes on [38;5;153mvisibilitychange[39m, so returning to the tab catches up immediately instead
  of up to 15s later.

  Does this structure look right before I go on to the data model and firing semantics?

